<Portfolio title="Experiments & archive"><section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">Earlier work / continuing lessons</p><h1>The experiment archive.</h1><p class="lead">Early tools that explored how agents could interact with Godot. Their lessons contributed to a broader development system.</p></section><section class="section archive-list"><article><span class="kicker">01 / Runtime access</span><h2>Godot Runtime Bridge</h2><p>GRB connected development agents to running Godot projects through a runtime bridge. Inspection, screenshots, and input made it possible to work from the game’s actual behavior.</p><p>It remains part of the history of the workflow. The current focus is the development harness built around real production.</p><a class="text-link" href="/ecosystem/grb/">Read the original GRB documentation →</a></article><article><span class="kicker">02 / Constrained prototyping</span><h2>Aesthetic Engine Builder</h2><p>AEB explored playable prototypes under strict visual and design constraints, including CRT-inspired wireframe graphics.</p><p>It was an experiment in directing implementation within a defined creative space. Today the portfolio leads with shipped games and the process used to build them.</p><a class="text-link" href="/ecosystem/builder/">Read the original AEB documentation →</a></article></section><section class="section archive-strip"><div><h2>From access to a process.</h2><p>The next step combines runtime tools with regression checks, evidence, and human review.</p></div><a class="text-link" href="/harness/">Explore the development harness →</a></section></Portfolio>
